= How to Play: Wave 1

A comprehensive deep-dive into Mass Effect mechanics for experienced
players. Assumes you have completed the trilogy at least once and
understand the fundamentals covered in the Wave 0 primer. Contains
mechanics spoilers (power combos, optimal builds) but no story spoilers.

== Power Combo System

Power combos are the most powerful offensive tool in Mass Effect. They
reward planning, timing, and squad composition. Mastering combos
transforms combat from a cover shooter into a chess match.

*Primer and detonator.* A combo requires two powers: a primer sets up
the target, and a detonator triggers the explosion. For example, use
Pull (primer) to lift an enemy, then Warp (detonator) to detonate the
lifted target for massive area damage. The detonator determines the
combo's damage -- invest points in your detonator powers.

*Tech combos.*
- *Tech Burst*: any tech primer (Overload, Energy Drain, Sabotage) plus
  any detonator. Deals electrical area damage and briefly stuns.
- *Fire Explosion*: Incinerate, Inferno Grenade, or any fire-primed
  target plus a detonator. Deals high fire area damage and applies a
  damage-over-time burn. Exceptionally strong against armor.
- *Cryo Explosion*: Cryo Blast, Cryo Ammo-primed targets, or Snap Freeze
  plus a detonator. Deals cold area damage and freezes nearby enemies.
  Frozen enemies shatter if killed while frozen.

*Biotic combos.*
- *Biotic Explosion*: any biotic primer (Pull, Singularity, Lift,
  Stasis, Reave) plus a biotic detonator (Warp, Throw, Shockwave,
  Cluster Grenade). Deals massive area damage and ragdolls nearby
  enemies. This is the core of Adept and Vanguard play.
- *Warp detonations* deserve special attention: Warp both primes AND
  detonates. A Warp-detonated combo deals bonus damage that ignores a
  portion of enemy armor. Use Warp as your detonator whenever possible.
- *Reave* is a special primer: it primes all nearby targets affected by
  the Reave area. A single Reave plus a cluster detonator like
  Shockwave can detonate an entire room.

*Combo damage scaling.* Combo damage scales with difficulty level and
the rank of your detonator power. On Insanity, combos are not optional --
they are your primary damage source, as enemy health pools are too
large for gunfire alone.

*Which powers prime, which detonate.* This varies by game. General rules:
- *Always primers*: Pull, Singularity, Cryo Blast, Inferno Grenade.
- *Always detonators*: Throw, Concussive Shot, Carnage, Cluster
  Grenade.
- *Can do both*: Warp, Overload, Incinerate, Shockwave, Energy Drain.

*Combo builds per class.*
- Adept: Singularity primes everything in range, Warp detonates.
  The classic combo loop.
- Sentinel: Overload strips shields, Warp primes armor, Throw detonates.
  Versatile against all defenses.
- Engineer: Overload primes shields, Incinerate primes armor, Overload
  again detonates. Tech combos for days.
- Vanguard: Pull primes, Charge detonates. High-risk, high-reward
  close-quarters combos.
- Infiltrator: Incinerate primes, sticky situations, squadmates detonate.
  You set them up, others knock them down.
- Soldier: no combo abilities. Use ammo powers to prime (Incendiary Ammo
  primes fire combos, Cryo Ammo primes cryo combos) and let squadmates
  detonate.

== Weapon Optimization

*Weapon tiers.* Each game uses a different upgrade system. In Mass
Effect 1, weapons have Roman numeral tiers (I through X) and
manufacturer variants. Higher-tier weapons have strictly better stats.
In Mass Effect 2 and 3, weapons have upgrade levels (1 through 5 or 10)
that improve damage, capacity, and accuracy. Always upgrade your
primary weapon to the highest available tier.

*Mod attachments.* Weapon mods are not optional -- they define your
weapon's role.
- *Assault rifles*: stability dampener, extended barrel (damage),
  magazine upgrade.
- *Sniper rifles*: high-velocity barrel (armor penetration), spare
  thermal clip, concentration mod (slow time while scoped).
- *Shotguns*: high-caliber barrel (damage), blade attachment (melee),
  spare thermal clip.
- *Pistols*: power magnifier (increases power damage -- great for
  Adepts and Sentinels), melee stunner, magazine upgrade.
- *SMGs*: high-velocity barrel, heat sink, ultralight materials.

*The power magnifier mod* (pistol) and the *omniblade* (pistol melee
stunner) are special: they are not just for weapon performance -- they
boost your other capabilities. Power magnifiers increase all power
damage, not just pistol damage. Caster classes should always carry
a pistol with a power magnifier.

*Ammo powers.* Choosing the right ammo power is critical:
- *Incendiary Ammo*: fire damage over time. Primes fire combos.
  Best against armor and organic enemies.
- *Cryo Ammo*: chance to freeze. Primes cryo combos. Best for crowd
  control and setting up shatter kills.
- *Disruptor Ammo*: bonus damage to shields and synthetics. Primes
  tech bursts. Best against Geth and shielded enemies.
- *Warp Ammo*: bonus damage to lifted or biotic-affected targets.
  Best paired with Adept squadmates who prime targets with biotics.
- *Armor-Piercing Ammo*: penetrates cover and armor. Best for Soldiers
  and Infiltrators facing armored enemies behind cover.

*Weapon weight vs. power cooldown (ME3).* In Mass Effect 3, every
weapon you carry adds to your weight total. Higher weight increases
power cooldown times. A power-focused Adept should carry only a
light pistol to minimize cooldowns. A Soldier can carry four heavy
weapons without concern. The weight bar in the loadout screen shows
your current cooldown bonus or penalty.

*Best-in-slot weapons per class.*
- Soldier: M-76 Revenant (assault rifle), M-300 Claymore (shotgun),
  Black Widow (sniper).
- Infiltrator: Black Widow, M-98 Widow, or Javelin (sniper).
- Adept: M-358 Talon (pistol -- shotgun-pistol hybrid) with power
  magnifier. Minimal weight loadout.
- Engineer: M-11 Wraith (shotgun) or M-358 Talon, plus a tech-boosting
  Omni-tool.
- Vanguard: M-300 Claymore or M-11 Wraith (shotgun). Charge plus
  point-blank shotgun is your entire combat loop.
- Sentinel: M-77 Paladin (heavy pistol) with power magnifier, plus
  a lightweight assault rifle for backup.

== Armor and Build Synergy

*Armor pieces.* In Mass Effect 1, armor is a full-body suit with mod
slots. In Mass Effect 2, armor pieces are individual (helmet,
shoulders, chest, arms, legs) with set bonuses. In Mass Effect 3,
you can mix and match pieces for a custom look and stat profile, or
wear a full set for a bonus.

*Build configurations.* Your armor choices should reinforce your
playstyle:
- *Power damage build*: prioritize power damage bonuses on armor
  pieces. Use the Inferno or Serrice Council armor sets.
- *Weapon damage build*: prioritize weapon damage and headshot damage.
  Use the Kestrel or Hahne-Kedar armor sets.
- *Survivability build*: prioritize shield strength, health, and
  damage resistance. The Kassa Fabrication or Blood Dragon armor
  sets excel here.

*Respeccing.* Mass Effect 2 and 3 let you respec your powers using a
research terminal on the Normandy (ME2) or the medbay (ME3). This
costs element zero or credits. Respec when you want to try a
different power evolution or when you acquire a weapon that changes
your build focus. Respeccing is cheap -- experiment freely.

== Economy and Resource Management

*Credits.* Credits are the universal currency across all three games.
In Mass Effect 1, credits are plentiful -- sell everything you do not
use. In Mass Effect 2, credits are tighter -- prioritize weapon and
armor upgrades before cosmetic purchases. In Mass Effect 3, credits
fund weapon upgrades at the Spectre Requisitions terminal.

*What to buy.*
- Weapon and armor upgrades at shops.
- Medi-gel capacity upgrades.
- Spectre-grade weapons (expensive but best-in-class).
- Ship upgrades in Mass Effect 2 (essential for the final mission).

*What to skip.*
- Cosmetic casual outfits (they do not affect gameplay).
- Low-tier guns when a better model is coming soon.
- Fish and model ships (unless you want the achievement).

*Mineral and resource farming.* In Mass Effect 2, planetary scanning
for minerals (element zero, iridium, platinum, palladium) funds your
upgrades. Element zero is the most important and rarest -- prioritize
planets rich in element zero. In Mass Effect 1, the Mako's mineral
deposits fund your gear. Scan everything.

*War Assets (ME3).* Your accumulated war assets determine the
available endings. Every side quest, scanned planet, and character
you helped contributes. More is always better. Without spoiling
specifics: aim for a high total, and prioritize assets that improve
the Crucible project.

== Insanity Difficulty

Insanity is the highest difficulty in Mass Effect. It is not just
"enemies have more health" -- the AI is more aggressive, enemies use
more powers, and every mistake is punished.

*What changes on Insanity.*
- Enemy health, shields, barriers, and armor are dramatically
  increased.
- Enemies are more aggressive, flank more often, and use grenades
  to flush you from cover.
- Enemy powers have shorter cooldowns and hit harder.
- Friendly fire is not a concern but the increased enemy damage means
  one mistake can kill you.

*Class-specific Insanity tactics.*
- *Soldier*: Adrenaline Rush is your best friend. Slow time, line up
  headshots, repeat. Use Incendiary Ammo to prime fire combos via
  squadmates.
- *Infiltrator*: Cloak, reposition, headshot, cloak again. You are a
  ghost. Prioritize high-damage single-shot sniper rifles.
- *Adept*: Stay behind cover at all times. Use Singularity to prime
  groups, Warp to detonate. Bring squadmates who can strip shields
  (enemies with shields cannot be lifted).
- *Engineer*: Drones and turrets draw enemy fire -- brilliant on
  Insanity. Overload shields, Incinerate armor, let your pet and
  squadmates do the shooting.
- *Vanguard*: Charge is dangerous on Insanity. Use Charge only on
  isolated, weakened enemies. Otherwise, play like a poor Adept
  from cover.
- *Sentinel*: Tech Armor for damage resistance, Overload for shields,
  Warp for armor, Throw for detonations. The ultimate swiss army knife.

*Squad composition.* On Insanity, bring squadmates who cover your
weaknesses. A Soldier needs shield-strippers (Garrus, Tali, EDI,
Kaiden). An Adept needs shield-strippers and a tank (Wrex, Grunt,
James, Ashley).

*Survivability strategies.*
- Shield-gating: when your shields break, you have a brief window
  of invulnerability. Use this window to reposition to new cover.
- Positioning: never stay in the same cover for more than a few
  seconds. Insanity enemies throw grenades at your position.
- Crowd control: a lifted or frozen enemy cannot shoot you. Prioritize
  crowd control powers over pure damage.

*Recommended bonus powers for Insanity.*
- Warp Ammo (universally useful).
- Energy Drain (free shield restore).
- Defense Matrix or Fortification (damage resistance for fragile
  classes).
- Dominate (turning an enemy to your side removes an attacker and
  adds a decoy). Only available with specific story choices.

== Squad Synergy

*Best squadmates per class.*
- Soldier: Liara (singularity crowd control) and Garrus (overload for
  shields). Your squad sets them up, you shoot them down.
- Adept: Garrus and Kaiden or Miranda. You need shield-stripping so
  your biotics can prime unshielded targets.
- Engineer: Wrex or Grunt (tank) and Liara (biotic primer for your
  tech detonations).
- Vanguard: Miranda (versatile, both Warp and Overload) and Thane
  (Warp for biotic combos, Shredder Ammo for weapon damage).
- Infiltrator: Liara (crowd control) and Tali or EDI (tech combos
  with your Incinerate).
- Sentinel: Jack (shockwave for detonating your Warp primers) and
  Zaeed or Ashley (pure weapon damage).

*Power combo partners.* The best squadmate pairings for combos:
- Liara plus Kaiden/Javik: both have Warp. You prime with Singularity
  (Liara), both detonate with Warp. Room-clearing biotic explosions.
- Garrus plus anyone with Incinerate: Garrus's Overload primes tech
  bursts, Incinerate detonates fire explosions. Toggle-level synergy.

*Squadmate power selection.* Turn off squadmate auto-use for powers
you want to trigger manually. A squadmate randomly casting Throw on
a shielded enemy wastes the cooldown. Direct their powers through the
power wheel for maximum effect.

== Import Mechanics

*What carries over between games.* When you import a save from one game
to the next, these carry over:
- Your character's first name, appearance, class, and origin/psych
  profile (though you can change class and appearance in ME2 and ME3).
- All major story decisions from previous games.
- Squadmate relationships and romance status.
- Bonus resources (credits, minerals, XP) based on your previous
  game's completion.

*Level cap and bonus start.*
- Importing a level 50-59 character from ME1 into ME2 starts you at
  level 3 with bonus credits and resources.
- Importing a level 60 character (requires two ME1 playthroughs) starts
  you at level 5 with even larger bonuses.
- Importing from ME2 into ME3 carries your level directly (up to level
  30) and grants bonus skill points based on your imported level.

*Key cross-game consequences.* Many decisions in ME1 have consequences
that only appear in ME2 or ME3. Some characters live or die based on
choices made two games earlier. Some factions remember what you did
and treat you accordingly. The most impactful decisions tend to involve
the fate of entire species or factions, so weigh your major choices
carefully. Without spoiling specifics: be thorough, be consistent, and
remember that mercy or ruthlessness in one game may pay dividends or
exact a price two games later.
